# Progressive install profiles — installer/README.md §Profiles. One
# `<profile><TAB><kit>` membership per line; `#` comments and blanks ignored.
#
# `full` has no rows here on purpose: it is every kit root in the payload,
# derived at run time. A hand-maintained "all the kits" roster is drift the day
# a kit lands.
#
# The rows below are the two rosters that are not derivable, each a judgment
# recorded with the criterion that produced it. That split is the whole contract
# of this file: **membership is hand-authored and shape is derived.** A row here
# says which kits a profile carries; nothing here says how the profiles relate,
# because that follows from the rows.
#
# The shape is a bounded lattice, not a chain: profiles are partially ordered by
# kit-set inclusion, exactly one sits below every other and exactly one above,
# and two profiles containing neither the other are alternatives rather than
# steps. The consumer smoke derives that order from these rows and the payload
# and asserts it — every named kit resolves, the bounds are unique, the maximum
# is the payload-derived profile, and moving up a comparable pair only ever adds
# to the gate roster. A fourth profile is admitted exactly when it fits; there is
# no count to raise here.

# starter — the smallest set that reaches a green battery with a working
# generated hook and at least one gate that reds on a real consumer defect
# needing no configuration outside the gates directory. gate-sdk is forced in:
# without it there is no runner, no hook generator, and no registry for a gate
# name to resolve against. It also already satisfies the last clause several
# times over, through gates that read the adopter's own tree rather than the
# vendored source — a tracked script missing its exec bit, a mutable action ref
# in their workflows, a malformed commit subject, a broken shell script under
# their gates directory. So any second kit would make the set larger than the
# criterion asks for, and the first experience is the framework, not a reading
# list.
starter	gate-sdk

# delegation — starter plus every kit whose subject is the agent session
# itself: the stage machine a session runs, the queue it selects work from, the
# evidence a stage must produce before it can close, the protocol it follows
# when it spawns a sub-session, the context budget it runs inside, the
# permission surface it acts through, and the delivery doctrine it follows.
#
# The honest consequence of the starting-subset rule, stated where the roster is
# read rather than only on the install page: lifecycle-kit registers **no gate**
# at install, because none of its gates is `zero-config` — for reasons that
# differ across the roster rather than one reason covering it
# (installer/README.md §What init seeds). So an adopter who picks this profile
# *for* lifecycle governance gets the stage machinery vendored and nothing
# enforcing it until the surface each gate reads exists — a stage stamp for
# most, the kit's own installer for two. Vendored is not yet enforced.
delegation	gate-sdk
delegation	lifecycle-kit
delegation	queue-kit
delegation	evidence-kit
delegation	delegation-kit
delegation	context-kit
delegation	guard-kit
delegation	doctrine-kit

# prose — the smallest set that governs a repository whose artifacts are prose,
# on a git-only floor. gate-sdk is forced in for the same reason it is forced in
# starter: no runner, no hook generator, no registry. canon-kit is the kit whose
# subject is authored documents; every other kit's subject is either the agent
# session — which is delegation's criterion, and why these two are alternatives
# rather than steps — or a deployed site.
#
# What the adopter actually receives, stated here because a criterion that
# overpromises is worse than none: link, claim, staleness and pointer governance
# over every README.md at any depth plus the agent file, which is real for a
# documentation repository since a docs tree is usually a tree of READMEs. It is
# not governance over `docs/*.md` — neither the manifest set nor the comment
# surface reads that path, and widening to it is the adopter's own
# CANON_KIT_PROSE_SURFACE_GLOBS edit in the config seam init already delivers.
prose	gate-sdk
prose	canon-kit

# What `full` adds on top of delegation is the set whose subject is something
# other than a session: canon-kit governs authored documents, drift-kit reports
# trends advisorily and registers no gates, site-kit governs a deployed docs
# site. canon-kit is the one that reads oddly at a glance and is deliberate —
# its subject is spec discipline over what a session produces, not how the
# session runs.
